---
import Card from "astro-theme-university/components/Card.astro";
import CardGrid from "astro-theme-university/components/CardGrid.astro";
import ContentLayout from "astro-theme-university/layouts/ContentLayout.astro";
import heroImage from "../assets/images/hero-home.avif";
import { courseMeta } from "../course-config";
import { sessionLabels, siteConfig } from "../site-config";

// STARTER_CONTENT: replace the hero artwork and its alt text below, then
// remove this comment.
---

<ContentLayout
  title={`${courseMeta.code}: ${courseMeta.title}`}
  description={courseMeta.description}
  heroImage={heroImage}
  heroImageAlt="A lecture theatre reduced to flat gold and black shapes, rows of seats sweeping past the frame, in a two-ink risograph print"
  {...siteConfig}
>
  <ul class="course-tags" aria-label="Course tags">
    {courseMeta.tags.map((tag) => <li>{tag}</li>)}
  </ul>

  <p>
    Everything can be made to run in a browser. That is not the interesting
    part. The interesting part is that a given program usually has several
    viable routes, the next program has a different several, and they differ
    from one another by orders of magnitude — in effort, in performance, and
    in what they leave you able to do afterwards. You have to choose between
    them before you have the evidence you would need to choose well.
  </p>

  <h2>What you will do</h2>
  <p>
    In week 2 you choose a program with no business running in a browser, and
    you keep it until week 12. Every week after that introduces another way it
    might be made to work — reimplementing its interfaces, compiling it,
    handing it a kernel-shaped surface, translating its instructions, or
    booting an operating system underneath it — and you work out what that way
    would cost you specifically.
  </p>
  <p>
    Proofs of concept are cheap now, so building them is the small half of the
    work. The large half is designing comparisons that settle something,
    committing on evidence you gathered yourself, and then — in the final
    assignment — giving yourself a new requirement and finding out what your
    commitment cost you.
  </p>
  <p>
    Sessions work differently. Each one applies the week's technique to a
    single program — yours where it fits, and one of the week's suggestions
    where it does not. No target suits all twelve techniques, and understanding
    the ones yours does not need is most of what the course is for.
  </p>

  <h2>Who it is for</h2>
  <p>
    Final-year students who can already program and are willing to be wrong in
    public. You should be comfortable reading a stack trace that crosses a
    language boundary, compiling something you did not write, and being told
    that your measurement does not support your conclusion.
  </p>
  <p>
    You do not need to have written WebAssembly, used a systems language, or
    known what a syscall is. You will need all three by about week 5.
  </p>

  <h2>What you leave with</h2>
  <p>
    A defensible answer to a question this discipline asks constantly and
    teaches almost nowhere: given several ways to build something, which one
    should you actually pick, and how would you know before it is too late to
    change your mind?
  </p>

  <h2>Where to go next</h2>
  <CardGrid columns={2}>
    <Card title="Lectures" href="/lectures/">
      <p>The twelve weeks, what each one covers, and what it ends on.</p>
    </Card>
    <Card title={sessionLabels.plural} href="/sessions/">
      <p>What happens in the room each week, and what to bring.</p>
    </Card>
    <Card title="Assessment" href="/assessments/">
      <p>Three assignments, what each is worth, and how they are marked.</p>
    </Card>
    <Card title="People" href="/people/">
      <p>Who teaches this course and how to reach them.</p>
    </Card>
  </CardGrid>
</ContentLayout>

<style>
  .course-tags {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: 0.5rem;
    margin-block: 0 2rem;
    padding: 0;
    list-style: none;
  }

  .course-tags li {
    padding: 0.25rem 0.65rem;
    border-radius: 999px;
    color: var(--at-text-secondary);
    background: var(--at-bg-alt);
    font-size: 0.9rem;
  }
</style>
